 picture i've  gone after wikipedia there's all kinds  of stuff on romanian history which i  import into link so i've cranked up my  linking my new words i'm up to 8 000 new  words in romanian  at least known words whatever that means  but but by and large if i now go to a  wikipedia article on romanian history  i know a lot of the words and the ones  that i don't know i look up and they are  going to my database at link and i will  eventually  know them so i've got a lot of  interesting  top down stuff happening  so far as the bottom up is concerned  before we had anything going at link  i bought uh pimsleur which gives you you  know a very repetitive and repeated  exposure to a small number of  phrases  um which is a form of bottom-up i bought  teach yourself so i was  going through the explanations there and  going through their dialogues and  picking up what i could pick up  and again i went on elance and i did up  i it must be i don't know  uh  40 episodes  of  you know sets of phrases  why because when would should present  future  uh  you know a whole bunch of stuff  that are patterns that i've put into our  link library for english  and now i've had translated into  romanian and i've had them recorded so i  also spend a fair amount of time  listening to these patterns so before i  go any further i will turn on my  handy  um  nano here and see where we are  we'll see what she says here  etc okay  okay  anyway i'll take this off now  so i mean i have heard those  hundred no not a hundred but lots and  lots of times so it's got all of the  although because however  uh greetings and stuff like that so  those are the bottom-up  uh bits and pieces  uh which i need to have a lot of  exposure to to drill them  into my brain and i prefer listening to  these because they are  first of all they're sentences and  patterns that i've written up because  they matter to me and i get to listen to  them over and over again  um  i prefer that to say pimsleur because  there's no english in these there's only  romanian and of course i have the  transcripts at link and i've saved all  the words and phrases and so i read them  again i listen to them again so there  i'm getting at the bits and pieces  but it would be boring if that's all i  did and my vocabulary would be limited  so i very much enjoy  reading  the wikipedia articles on romanian  history and listening to  the interviews on radio  romania and i would say you know i've  been at romania for three weeks uh a  week ago i couldn't understand much now  i think i understand about 50  there was the discussion there about the  byzantine empire and the influence on  romania and  you know the influence of the west  versus byzantium and so forth and so on  uh there was another discussion about uh  you know uh  what was this king king charles i guess  who was the uh holland zolan  german  prince who became the king of romania  around the time i guess of the first  world war i miss a lot but it's fun  and now i'm going to get these  transcribed elance so  i very much recommend  that your strategy would be one of going  after more difficult but more  interesting texts  to give your brain exposure to  the big picture but do it in an  environment  like link where you can actually read  what you're listening to look up words  that you don't know  and work on learning those  and i will sometimes review the flash  cards on on my iphone if i'm waiting  somewhere  and then vary that with easier content  where you are basically getting repeated  exposure to some of the fundamental  patterns of the language some of the  fundamental  structures  and some of the useful connecting words  and and whatever that you need in the  language  now  the way languages are taught you know  it's interesting i think  that most of the  um  i call them the youtube polyglots that i  know of the richard simcots the lucas  even  you know people from you know historical  importance like cat olam the famous  hungarian uh polyglot lady  their emphasis has been more on the top  down  on the uh the input  which doesn't mean that they you know  neglected the other but they did that  primarily and and the more they did that  i think the better they became in their  languages  however most instruction  focuses on the bits and pieces and and i  think the reason there is clear that for  a learner the most pleasing thing to do  the easiest thing to do is input  and uh for many learners at least it's  interesting it's easier to do it's it's  portable it's uh  less expensive  but and from a teacher's perspective  it's easier to teach the nuts and bolts  it gives you something that you can try  to teach  however unfortunately with the nuts and  bolts  that no matter how many times i read  explanations of grammar like in my  romanian book i'm reading them now for  the i don't know umpteenth time they  don't necessarily stick until a certain  point where we've i've seen them so  often in other  settings in other contexts that finally  it clicks in  so the deliberate study of the nuts and  bolts the deliberate attempt to teach  the nuts and bolts is doomed to failure  if there isn't at least twice as much  time  spent with  the big picture the input and again  getting back to the  my sort of the three keys it's not mine  i heard this from a lady at san diego  san diego state university you know  attitude time and the ability to notice  well if you're doing things that are  interesting you're going to have a more  positive attitude  uh your attitude towards the language is  going to be more positive  uh  in order to put in the time you need to  do things that are interesting  so  going after  the big picture interesting content  deals with the attitude and the time  issue  the noticing requires a combination of  both if you just expose yourself to  input  contrary to all the  research that crash and others have done  i don't buy it i know that there are  things that i just won't notice unless  it's pointed out to me  pointed out to me perhaps in a grammar  explanation or because i hear these  basic patterns over and over and over  again until finally oh yeah that relates  to what i've been you know noticing  or i start to notice it so  to conclude  uh my suggestion at least the strategy  that works for me and you don't have to  do what i say obviously and you won't  however you know i do have some  experience in language learning perhaps  more than the average language learner  and some degree of success  here i am with my romanian i've been at  it for three weeks  uh  obviously i'm helped by the fact that  the vocabulary a lot of it is very  similar to romance languages but i feel  very confident that  you know i've got a month to go before i  go to romania  and the radio romania um  you know podcasts are becoming clearer  and clearer to me  uh it's becoming easier and easier to  read i've only been out for threes uh  you know and i find myself now because  i'm also starting to speak with uh you  know tutors  that i'm able to start using some of  these patterns that i've been listening  to over and over that kind of been  dripping on my brain so i very much  recommend strategy of both  top down and bottom up but at least two  or three times as much  uh top down as bottom up with the uh  with the caveat that when you are  starting out the first of the while you  need more of the bottom up because you  can't access the top down it just so  happens in romania and i can do that i  don't think you know i know i couldn't  have done that in czech  because the vocabulary would be would be  too different for me so there it is  uh strategy  top down say three parts top down and  one part bottom up i look forward to  hearing your comments bye for now

